# The operations that both draw and induce. One row per (operation, layer drawn from, layer
# committed to): an operation that consumes from one layer while committing on another links the
# two, and joining draws to inductions on the operation they share finds it.
#
# The links compose; the quantities do not
#   Multiply the two amounts and the answer comes out in people·launches, which is not a rate of
#   anything, because each layer carries its own unit. So this relation reports which layer reaches
#   which, and leaves the numbers beside it unmultiplied. There is no count of how often an
#   operation fires either: sequence and timing are BPMN's job, and this model leaves them to it.
#
# Empty for want of a measurement
#   In this corpus every operation that qualifies draws an amount nobody measured, so the product
#   cannot be worked out at all. The column that would show how layers drive one another is empty
#   because the quantity that matters has no measurement behind it, which is the thing this model
#   exists to be able to say.
-- the shared index is (filing, operation); pm:Operation carries both children.
SELECT d.filing, d.operation,
       d.layer AS drawn_from, d.mode AS drawn,
       n.layer AS commits,    n.mode AS committed,
       coalesce(d.unit, '(unmeasured)') || ' * '
    || coalesce(n.unit, '(unmeasured)') AS the_unit_that_warns_you,
       d.mode * n.mode                    AS the_product_nobody_should_use
FROM      (
    :compose(entries/draws.sqlc)
) d
JOIN      (
    :compose(entries/inductions.sqlc)
) n USING (filing, operation)
